//? Title: INLA: Conditional Gaussians and Hyperparameter Mixing
//? Subtitle: Exact controls, skew approximation, bounded quadrature and a penguin mixed model
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Observations may depend on latent effects whose prior scales are themselves unknown.
Inference must account for uncertainty in both the effects and those scales, often
without a tractable posterior integral. Integrated nested Laplace approximation
replaces difficult conditional posteriors with local approximations and integrates
low-dimensional hyperparameters numerically. We study this library's dense Gaussian
strategy through an exact three-observation normal model, a skew Bernoulli posterior,
and an explicit Gaussian mixture over uncertain precision. Grid refinement and a
Palmer penguins mixed model expose numerical and prior sensitivity. A separate
multiple-testing appendix preserves the original p-value example with its rejection
counts corrected; it is not part of Bayesian posterior inference.
)
\toc

> The method

>> Specify the latent Gaussian model and the approximation

Let \(x\in\mathbb R^p\) be the latent field, \(\theta\in\mathbb R^d\) its
hyperparameters, and \(D\) the observation design. The model is

$$
 \eta=Dx,\qquad x\mid\theta\sim N(0,Q(\theta)^{-1}),\qquad
 p(x,\theta\mid y)\propto p(\theta)|Q(\theta)|^{1/2}
 \exp(-x^TQ(\theta)x/2)\prod_i p(y_i\mid\eta_i,\theta).
$$
\alttext(The observation predictor is D times the latent field. Given hyperparameters, the field has a zero-mean normal prior with precision Q. Its posterior combines the hyperprior, Gaussian prior normalization and quadratic, and independent observation likelihoods.)

The likelihood callback supplies its log density, score \(s_i=\partial\log p_i/\partial\eta_i\),
and nonnegative curvature \(c_i=-\partial^2\log p_i/\partial\eta_i^2\).
At fixed \(\theta\), the gradient and negative Hessian in the latent field are

$$
 g=D^Ts-Qx,\qquad H=Q+D^T\operatorname{diag}(c_i)D,\qquad
 x_{\mathrm{next}}=x+\alpha H^{-1}g.
$$
\alttext(The latent posterior score is the transposed design times likelihood scores minus prior precision times x. Its negative Hessian adds the likelihood curvature to Q. A safeguarded Newton step solves H delta equals g and scales that step by alpha.)

Positive-definite \(Q\) and nonnegative curvature make \(H\) positive definite,
so the conditional log posterior is strictly concave. A stationary point, if attained,
is its unique mode. The solver uses Cholesky solves and step halving. It stops on a
small *proposed* Newton step relative to \(1+\|x\|_\infty\); exhaustion or a failed
line search is rejected. The final score norm is exposed, but numerical stopping alone
does not quantify the Gaussian approximation error.

At the conditional mode \(m(\theta)\), use
\(x\mid y,\theta\approx N(m,H^{-1})\). This is exact for a normal likelihood with
linear predictor and known hyperparameters. For a non-Gaussian likelihood, the mode
usually differs from the actual conditional posterior mean.

>> The Laplace ratio and the outer integral

The normalized Gaussian prior contributes \(|Q|^{1/2}(2\pi)^{-p/2}\).
Integrating the local Gaussian contributes \((2\pi)^{p/2}|H|^{-1/2}\), so those
\(2\pi\) factors cancel. Thus the approximate log hyperposterior integrand is

$$
 \ell(\theta)=\log p(\theta)+\frac12\log|Q(\theta)|
 +\sum_i\log p(y_i\mid D_im,\theta)-\frac12m^TQ(\theta)m
 -\frac12\log|H(\theta)|.
$$
\alttext(The approximate log hyperposterior is log hyperprior plus half the log determinant of Q, plus log likelihood at the conditional mode, minus the prior quadratic and half the log determinant of the conditional negative Hessian.)

A seeded differential-evolution search finds a candidate mode inside a declared box;
it is not a global-optimality certificate. Finite differences estimate hyperparameter
curvature there. Its inverse determines a rotated, equally spaced grid in curvature-SD
coordinates. If the hyper-Hessian is unusable, the recorded fallback uses diagonal
covariance equal to one sixteenth of each box width squared. Small covariance
eigenvalues are floored for numerical grid construction.

For one hyperparameter, cells are clipped to the box and evaluated at their clipped
midpoints, with their actual widths. In higher dimensions, rotated cells retain a
constant volume and outside-box centers are skipped: boundary quadrature is coarser
and needs separate refinement. All retained conditional fits must converge. The
reported skipped-point count can include both outside support and failed evaluations;
inspect the model and sensitivity rather than interpreting it as a numerical error bound.

The retained TikZ source needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm. Normal exports
consume the committed SVG and do not rerun those tools.

```{bash, file="figures/inla_nested_gaussians.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/inla_nested_gaussians.tikz > build/inla_nested_gaussians.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/inla_nested_gaussians.svg build/inla_nested_gaussians.dvi > build/inla_nested_gaussians.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the conditional-Gaussian and hyperparameter-mixture diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the conditional-Gaussian and hyperparameter-mixture diagram.
// \image(figures/inla_nested_gaussians.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The inner Gaussian approximation supplies both an effect distribution and the outer grid's Laplace score.)
\alttext(At each fixed theta, compute the latent mode and Hessian, score the hyperparameter, and normalize score times cell volume into weights. The final marginal is a mixture of conditional normals, with within-component and between-component variance.)

>> Combine moments without confusing them with a single Gaussian

With quadrature cell measures \(\Delta_k\), let
\(w_k=\exp(\ell_k)\Delta_k/\sum_l\exp(\ell_l)\Delta_l\). Then

$$
 \bar m_j=\sum_kw_km_{kj},\qquad
 V_j=\underbrace{\sum_kw_k[H_k^{-1}]_{jj}}_{\text{within}}
 +\underbrace{\sum_kw_k(m_{kj}-\bar m_j)^2}_{\text{between}},\qquad
 \widetilde p(x_j\mid y)=\sum_kw_k\phi(x_j;m_{kj},[H_k^{-1}]_{jj}).
$$
\alttext(The marginal mean averages conditional modes. Total variance is average conditional variance plus variance of conditional modes. The marginal density is the weighted mixture of conditional normal densities, which need not be normal.)

The between term is nonnegative. This proves total variance exceeds its *own weighted
within term*, not that it exceeds the variance of a different fit evaluated only at
the hyperparameter mode. Empirical Bayes (EB) retains that single conditional fit,
sets theta uncertainty and between variance to zero, and omits the outer integral.
Its reported integrated evidence is therefore NaN. With a normalized likelihood,
proper normalized priors and intentional integration support, the grid's
\(\log\sum_k\exp(\ell_k)\Delta_k\) approximates log evidence. An arbitrary
constant improper prior makes its scale arbitrary.

This implementation follows the **Gaussian latent-marginal strategy**: it does not
perform INLA's simplified/full marginal Laplace skew correction, sparse GMRF algebra,
adaptive integration or exact posterior sampling. Conditional Newton work is
\(O(np^2+p^3)\) per iteration; retaining \(K\) covariance matrices costs
\(O(Kp^2)\). Tensor-grid cost grows as \(K=m^d\), so Grid caps \(d\) at four and
candidates at one million. These are numerical limits, not promises of practical
performance at those extremes.

> Small worked examples

>> Three normal observations: every conditional calculation is exact

Take \(y=(1,2,3)\), \(y_i\mid x\sim N(x,1)\), and \(x\sim N(0,1)\).
The likelihood as a function of \(x\) is proportional to \(N(2,1/3)\).
Completing the square gives posterior precision \(1+3=4\), mean
\((1+2+3)/4=1.5\), and variance \(1/4\). Starting at zero, the Newton score is six,
so the first step is \(6/4=1.5\); the next proposed step is zero.

The normalized evidence is also hand-checkable. The marginal covariance of \(y\)
is \(I+\mathbf1\mathbf1^T\), with determinant four and inverse
\(I-\mathbf1\mathbf1^T/4\), giving
\(\log p(y)=-3\log(2\pi)/2-\log(4)/2-5/2\).
The normalized likelihood-shaped curve below is an aid to reading the completed
square; it is not itself a posterior.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/inla_normal_exact.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace b           = dm::bayes;
  using M               = dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>;
  const auto likelihood = b::inla_gaussian_likelihood([](const auto&) { return 1.; });
  const auto fit        = b::INLA().conditional(M(3, 1, 1), {1, 2, 3}, likelihood, M(1, 1, 1));
  const auto reference  = b::inla_scalar_reference({1, 2, 3}, likelihood, 1, {}, -6, 8, 4001);
  const auto x          = dm::seql(-1.5, 4.5, 401).values();
  std::vector<double> prior, likelihood_shape, posterior;
  for (double v : x) {
    prior.push_back(std::exp(b::normal_lpdf(v, 0, 1)));
    likelihood_shape.push_back(std::exp(b::normal_lpdf(v, 2, 1 / std::sqrt(3.))));
    posterior.push_back(std::exp(b::normal_lpdf(v, fit.mode[0], std::sqrt(fit.covariance(0, 0)))));
  }
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                  .line(x, prior, "prior", {130, 130, 130})
                  .line(x, likelihood_shape, "likelihood shape", {190, 110, 35})
                  .line(x, posterior, "posterior", {40, 100, 190})
                  .title("Exact normal-normal posterior")
                  .x_label("latent mean x")
                  .y_label("density")
                  .x_limits(-1.5, 4.5)
                  .y_limits(0, 1.1)
                  .legend(
                      {"prior", "likelihood shape", "posterior"},
                      {{130, 130, 130}, {190, 110, 35}, {40, 100, 190}});
  plot.size(850, 490).save_svg("build/inla_normal_exact.svg");
  std::print(
      "Conditional Gaussian\n\n{}\n\nDirect finite-window reference: mean={:.9f}, SD={:.9f}, log "
      "evidence={:.9f}, edge mass={:.3g}.\n",
      b::inla_conditional_table(fit).to_md(6),
      reference.mean[0],
      reference.sd[0],
      reference.log_evidence,
      reference.edge_mass);
}
```
