| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 7 | 5 | 7 | 1.602872 | 0.572186 | -0.471210 | 0.804326 | -6.003018 | 0.000073 | 0 |
| 15 | 5 | 15 | 1.600192 | 0.571974 | -0.468929 | 0.815222 | -6.005825 | 0.000031 | 0 |
| 31 | 5 | 31 | 1.600196 | 0.571969 | -0.468937 | 0.815182 | -6.005829 | 0.000015 | 0 |
| 61 | 5 | 61 | 1.600198 | 0.571966 | -0.468941 | 0.815157 | -6.005832 | 0.000007 | 0 |
| 61 | 7 | 61 | 1.600189 | 0.571978 | -0.468921 | 0.815258 | -6.005822 | 0.000000 | 0 |

\image(figures/inla_grid_refinement.svg)
// result_end
\caption(With Gaussian conditional posteriors, changes along this refinement sequence isolate hyperparameter quadrature and mode-search effects.)
\alttext(Two panels show latent mean and SD, and approximate log evidence, at 7, 15, 31 and 61 grid points, plus a wider 61-point span. The table also records theta moments, retained cells, skipped candidates and edge mass.)

> Observed data: Palmer penguins

The bundled 344-row dataset includes sex, body mass, species and island. Retain rows
complete in sex, body_mass_g and island, giving 333 observations. Define male as one
and use centered body mass in kilograms, \(\text{mass}=\text{body_mass_g}/1000-4.2\).
Fit `male ~ mass + (1 | island)`, retaining the original report's random-intercept
structure while making units and priors explicit. This is a descriptive fit to all
complete rows; there is no held-out prediction-performance claim.

Fixed coefficients receive independent \(N(0,2.5^2)\) priors on these centered units.
The island random-intercept SD \(\lambda\) has a normalized uniform prior on
[0.01,3]. The latter is a density on SD itself, not on variance or log SD. General
random-slope models use independent uniform priors on packed covariance-factor
entries, not independent uniform variance/correlation priors. With three islands,
prior support for an SD matters and will be checked separately.

Species composition differs sharply by island; the formula omits species. Consequently
an island coefficient can absorb species composition and other omitted structure.
It should not be read as a causal island effect. Neither descriptive fitting accuracy
nor a local Gaussian interval resolves that model limitation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/inla_penguins.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d =
      dm::datasets::penguins()
          .filter([](const auto& r) {
            return !r.is_null("sex") && !r.is_null("body_mass_g") && !r.is_null("island");
          })
          .mutate_with(
              "male", [](const auto& r) { return r.get_string("sex") == "male" ? 1. : 0.; })
          .mutate_with(
              "mass", [](const auto& r) { return r.get_double("body_mass_g") / 1000 - 4.2; });
  dm::stats::INLAMixedModelOptions o;
  o.family                = dm::stats::INLAMixedModelFamily::Binomial;
  o.fixed_effect_prior_sd = 2.5;
  o.lambda_diag_epsilon   = .01;
  o.lambda_bound          = 3;
  o.grid_points_per_dim   = 41;
  o.grid_span             = 5;
  o.mode_population_size  = 20;
  o.mode_max_generations  = 80;
  const dm::stats::INLAMixedModel model(d, "male ~ mass + (1 | island)", o);
  const auto                      effects   = model.random_effects_frame();
  auto                            intervals = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < effects.nrows(); ++j) {
    intervals.line(
        {double(j), double(j)},
        {effects.double_at("q025", j), effects.double_at("q975", j)},
        "",
        {40, 100, 190});
    intervals.points({double(j)}, {effects.double_at("mean", j)}, "", {40, 100, 190}, 6);
  }
  intervals.title("Island intercepts: mixture intervals")
      .x_label("island")
      .x_ticks({0, 1, 2}, model.group_labels())
      .y_label("log-odds random intercept")
      .x_limits(-.5, 2.5)
      .y_limits(-5, 5)
      .abline_h(0, {130, 130, 130}, 1);
  auto                                 curves = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const auto                           mass   = dm::seql(2.7, 6.3, 121).values();
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors{{40, 100, 190}, {190, 100, 35}, {40, 140, 80}};
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < model.num_groups(); ++j) {
    dm::dstruct::DataFrame query;
    std::vector<double>    centered;
    for (double v : mass)
      centered.push_back(v - 4.2);
    query.add_column("mass", centered);
    query.add_column("island", std::vector<std::string>(mass.size(), model.group_labels()[j]));
    curves.line(mass, model.predict(query), model.group_labels()[j], colors[j]);
  }
  curves.title("Plug-in curves at fixed island")
      .x_label("body mass (kg)")
      .y_label("inverse-link mean-field prediction")
      .x_limits(2.7, 6.3)
      .y_limits(0, 1.35)
      .legend(model.group_labels(), colors);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
      .add(intervals)
      .add(curves)
      .size(1100, 490)
      .fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/inla_penguins.svg");
  std::print(
      "{} complete rows; {} islands.\n\nIsland/species composition\n\n{}\n\nFixed "
      "effects\n\n{}\n\nRandom effects\n\n{}\n\nPhysical SD moments\n\n{}\n\nRetained cells={}, "
      "skipped={}, hyper-curvature fallback={}, grid-edge mass={:.3g}, log evidence={:.9f}.\n",
      model.observations(),
      model.num_groups(),
      (d | dm::group_by("island", "species") |
       dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("rows"), dm::agg::mean("male", "male_fraction")) | dm::to_md(4)),
      model.fixed_effects_frame().to_md(),
      effects.to_md(),
      model.variance_components_frame().to_md(),
      model.posterior().theta_grid_weights.size(),
      model.posterior().grid_skipped_points,
      model.posterior().grid_covariance_fallback,
      model.posterior().grid_edge_mass,
      model.log_marginal_likelihood());
}
```
