\image(figures/inla_penguins.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Dots are approximate mixture means; intervals are its equal-tail 95% quantiles. Prediction curves hold island fixed while varying mass.)
\alttext(Left: Torgersen, Biscoe and Dream random-intercept mixture means with equal-tail 95% approximate posterior intervals and a zero reference. Right: inverse-link predictions at the posterior mean latent field, one curve per island, as body mass varies.)

The interval endpoints come from the actual retained Gaussian mixture CDF, not a
symmetric mean-plus-or-minus-two-SD shortcut. They remain intervals of an approximation.
`variance_components_frame()` transforms each grid point before computing the mean
and SD of physical random-effect SDs. By contrast, legacy `random_effect_std_devs()`
evaluates the transformation at mean theta; those quantities can differ for multivariate
factors. Gaussian `residual_std_dev()` similarly returns exp(mean log SD), which is
not the mean residual SD.

`predict()` computes the inverse link at the posterior mean latent field. For logit
and log links this is generally different from integrating the response mean over the
posterior. Unseen/null groups set their random effects to zero; that fallback does
not integrate the distribution of a new island. The smooth curves therefore illustrate
the fitted mean field rather than calibrated posterior predictive probabilities.

>> Separate grid sensitivity from prior sensitivity

First change only the number of grid points, holding the [0.01,3] SD prior fixed.
Then fix 81 grid points and vary the prior's upper bound through 1.5, 3 and 5. This
second experiment changes the model, not just numerical resolution. Do not choose a
prior bound by maximizing these observed-data summaries or evidence values. With
only three groups, movement in the random-effect SD is scientifically relevant even
when the fixed mass slope moves little. A zero outer-grid edge mass cannot establish
that a deliberately finite prior bound is unimportant.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/inla_penguins_sensitivity.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d =
      dm::datasets::penguins()
          .filter([](const auto& r) {
            return !r.is_null("sex") && !r.is_null("body_mass_g") && !r.is_null("island");
          })
          .mutate_with(
              "male", [](const auto& r) { return r.get_string("sex") == "male" ? 1. : 0.; })
          .mutate_with(
              "mass", [](const auto& r) { return r.get_double("body_mass_g") / 1000 - 4.2; });
  std::vector<std::string> experiment;
  std::vector<double>      grid, bound, slope, mean_sd, sd_of_sd, edge;
  for (const auto& setting : std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, double>>{
           {11, 3}, {21, 3}, {41, 3}, {81, 3}, {81, 1.5}, {81, 5}}) {
    dm::stats::INLAMixedModelOptions o;
    o.family                = dm::stats::INLAMixedModelFamily::Binomial;
    o.fixed_effect_prior_sd = 2.5;
    o.lambda_diag_epsilon   = .01;
    o.lambda_bound          = setting.second;
    o.grid_points_per_dim   = setting.first;
    o.grid_span             = 5;
    o.mode_population_size  = 20;
    o.mode_max_generations  = 80;
    const dm::stats::INLAMixedModel model(d, "male ~ mass + (1 | island)", o);
    const auto                      components = model.variance_components_frame();
    experiment.push_back(setting.first == 81 && setting.second != 3 ? "prior" : "grid");
    grid.push_back(setting.first);
    bound.push_back(setting.second);
    slope.push_back(model.fixed_effects_mean()[1]);
    mean_sd.push_back(components.double_at("mean_sd", 0));
    sd_of_sd.push_back(components.double_at("sd_of_sd", 0));
    edge.push_back(model.posterior().grid_edge_mass);
  }
  auto refinement = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                        .line(
                            {grid.begin(), grid.begin() + 4},
                            {mean_sd.begin(), mean_sd.begin() + 4},
                            "",
                            {40, 100, 190})
                        .points(
                            {grid.begin(), grid.begin() + 4},
                            {mean_sd.begin(), mean_sd.begin() + 4},
                            "",
                            {40, 100, 190},
                            6)
                        .title("Numerical refinement: same prior")
                        .x_label("grid points per axis")
                        .y_label("posterior mean island SD");
  auto priors = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                    .points(
                        {bound[4], bound[3], bound[5]},
                        {mean_sd[4], mean_sd[3], mean_sd[5]},
                        "",
                        {190, 100, 35},
                        7)
                    .line(
                        {bound[4], bound[3], bound[5]},
                        {mean_sd[4], mean_sd[3], mean_sd[5]},
                        "",
                        {190, 100, 35})
                    .title("Prior support: different models")
                    .x_label("uniform SD prior upper bound")
                    .y_label("posterior mean island SD");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
      .add(refinement)
      .add(priors)
      .size(1100, 490)
      .fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/inla_penguins_sensitivity.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("experiment", experiment);
  table.add_column("axis_points", grid);
  table.add_column("SD_prior_upper", bound);
  table.add_column("mass_slope", slope);
  table.add_column("mean_island_sd", mean_sd);
  table.add_column("sd_of_island_sd", sd_of_sd);
  table.add_column("outer_grid_edge_mass", edge);
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(6));
}
```
