| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| grid | 11 | 3.0 | 2.455886 | 1.794508 | 0.602423 | 0 |
| grid | 21 | 3.0 | 2.458160 | 1.789600 | 0.603998 | 0 |
| grid | 41 | 3.0 | 2.458321 | 1.790849 | 0.604980 | 0 |
| grid | 81 | 3.0 | 2.458316 | 1.790941 | 0.605403 | 0 |
| prior | 81 | 1.5 | 2.398713 | 1.148209 | 0.231118 | 0 |
| prior | 81 | 5.0 | 2.473804 | 2.291977 | 1.059059 | 0 |

\image(figures/inla_penguins_sensitivity.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The left panel changes quadrature resolution within one model. The right changes a normalized prior and therefore the model itself.)
\alttext(Left: mean island SD under 11, 21, 41 and 81 grid points with prior upper bound three. Right: mean island SD under prior upper bounds 1.5, three and five, each using 81 grid points.)

> Independent appendix: adjust the original ten p-values

The supplied p-values are \((.001,.004,.009,.03,.12,.18,.35,.51,.72,.98)\).
They are illustrative numbers, not tests extracted from the penguin posterior, and
small p-values alone do not identify which null hypotheses are true. For ordered
values \(p_{(i)}\), Bonferroni uses \(\min(1,mp_i)\); Holm takes the running
maximum of \((m-j+1)p_{(j)}\) through rank \(i\), capped at one. BH takes the
backward running minimum of \(mp_{(j)}/j\) from rank \(i\), also capped at one.
Both procedures restore the caller's original order.

At level .05, Bonferroni and Holm reject **two**, while BH rejects **three**.
The fourth raw value .03 is not rejected by any of them: its adjusted values are
.30, .21 and .075. Bonferroni/Holm control family-wise error under valid marginal
p-values without a dependence restriction. BH controls false discovery rate under
independence or suitable positive dependence. FDR is an expected false-discovery
proportion with the zero-rejection case defined as zero, not a guarantee for each
realized list. These procedures do not convert an approximate Bayesian interval
into a multiplicity-adjusted frequentist test.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/inla_p_adjust.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<double> raw{.001, .004, .009, .03, .12, .18, .35, .51, .72, .98};
  const auto                bonf = dm::stats::p_adjust(raw, dm::stats::PAdjustMethod::Bonferroni),
             holm                = dm::stats::p_adjust(raw, dm::stats::PAdjustMethod::Holm),
             bh                  = dm::stats::p_adjust(raw, dm::stats::PAdjustMethod::BH);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("raw", raw);
  table.add_column("Bonferroni", bonf);
  table.add_column("Holm", holm);
  table.add_column("BH", bh);
  auto       all = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), detail = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const auto rank = dm::seql(1, 10, 10).values();
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors{
      {110, 110, 110}, {190, 70, 40}, {40, 100, 190}, {35, 140, 75}};
  const std::vector<std::string>         labels{"raw", "Bonf", "Holm", "BH"};
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> series{raw, bonf, holm, bh};
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < series.size(); ++j) {
    all.line(rank, series[j], labels[j], colors[j]);
    detail.line({1, 2, 3, 4}, {series[j].begin(), series[j].begin() + 4}, labels[j], colors[j]);
  }
  all.title("All ten supplied p-values")
      .x_label("hypothesis rank")
      .y_label("p-value")
      .x_limits(1, 10)
      .y_limits(0, 1.5)
      .abline_h(.05, {100, 100, 100}, 1)
      .legend(labels, colors);
  detail.title("First four hypotheses")
      .x_label("hypothesis rank")
      .y_label("p-value")
      .x_limits(1, 4)
      .y_limits(0, .5)
      .abline_h(.05, {100, 100, 100}, 1)
      .legend(labels, colors);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(all).add(detail).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/inla_p_adjust.svg");
  std::print(
      "Bonferroni rejects {}; Holm rejects {}; BH rejects {} at .05.\n\n{}\n",
      std::count_if(bonf.begin(), bonf.end(), [](double p) { return p <= .05; }),
      std::count_if(holm.begin(), holm.end(), [](double p) { return p <= .05; }),
      std::count_if(bh.begin(), bh.end(), [](double p) { return p <= .05; }),
      table.to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Bonferroni rejects 2; Holm rejects 2; BH rejects 3 at .05.

| raw | Bonferroni | Holm | BH |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.001 | 0.01 | 0.010 | 0.0100 |
| 0.004 | 0.04 | 0.036 | 0.0200 |
| 0.009 | 0.09 | 0.072 | 0.0300 |
| 0.030 | 0.30 | 0.210 | 0.0750 |
| 0.120 | 1.00 | 0.720 | 0.2400 |
| 0.180 | 1.00 | 0.900 | 0.3000 |
| 0.350 | 1.00 | 1.000 | 0.5000 |
| 0.510 | 1.00 | 1.000 | 0.6375 |
| 0.720 | 1.00 | 1.000 | 0.8000 |
| 0.980 | 1.00 | 1.000 | 0.9800 |

\image(figures/inla_p_adjust.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The corrected rejection counts are two for Bonferroni, two for Holm and three for BH. The fourth hypothesis is not rejected under any method.)
\alttext(Left: all ten raw and adjusted p-values. Right: the first four values with a .05 reference line, showing that rank three passes BH only and rank four passes none.)

> Conclusion

The normal-normal control verifies the inner mode, covariance and evidence calculation
against exact algebra. The Bernoulli control separates Newton convergence from local
Gaussian accuracy. Unknown precision demonstrates genuine hyperparameter mixing,
with the full conditional grid and within/between variance visible. Grid refinement
checks the numerical integration, while the penguin prior experiment changes the
model and exposes sensitivity that numerical convergence cannot remove.

These are approximate Gaussian-mixture marginals from a dense implementation, not
exact posteriors or the full R-INLA marginal correction machinery. Posterior SDs,
mixture quantiles, transformed physical SD moments and inverse-link plug-in predictions
are distinct quantities. Keeping those distinctions explicit makes the penguin example
interpretable, including the limits imposed by three islands and omitted species.
The p-value appendix is a separate frequentist calculation with its own error-rate
assumptions and the original report's rejection-count errors corrected.

> References

1. Rue, H., Martino, S. and Chopin, N. (2009). “Approximate Bayesian inference for latent
   Gaussian models by using integrated nested Laplace approximations.” *JRSS B* 71,
   319–392. [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9868.2008.00700.x).
   The present implementation uses Gaussian conditional marginals without the paper's
   further individual-marginal Laplace corrections.
2. Tierney, L. and Kadane, J. B. (1986). “Accurate Approximations for Posterior Moments
   and Marginal Densities.” *JASA* 81, 82–86.
   [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1986.10478240).
3. [R stats: p.adjust](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/p.adjust.html),
   including the original references to Holm (1979) and Benjamini–Hochberg (1995) and
   their dependence assumptions.
4. Gorman, K. B., Williams, T. D. and Fraser, W. R. (2014). “Ecological Sexual Dimorphism
   and Environmental Variability within a Community of Antarctic Penguins.” *PLOS ONE*
   9, e90081. [Original data study](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0090081).
   Dataset structure: [palmerpenguins](https://allisonhorst.github.io/palmerpenguins/).

